{
  "version": 6,
  "description": "Products in scope (wa6n). Each entry has a class. channel: coding agents and IDEs, MCP servers and clients of any publisher, skills, plugins and extensions; any level. catalog: SDKs, libraries and developer tools; notice at most, never a real-time post. out: operating systems, browsers, runtimes and infrastructure; a new advisory starts no thread, and an existing thread still takes updates. channel_if (optional, on a catalog or out entry): \"code-execution\" lets a reading of that entry that shows remote code execution post at high, never critical (spec/ruleset.json). Components take their entry's class. match: exact identities, lowercase and trimmed. A product text resolves to an entry only when the whole text, NFKC-normalised, lowercased, with Unicode dashes as '-' and whitespace runs as one space, equals one identity; nothing is matched by a word, a prefix or a substring, so claude-code-router is not Claude Code. An identity is a product name, a repository slug (owner/repo), a package key (ecosystem:name, the form a collector finding's package field takes) or CISA KEV's vendorProject and product joined by one space. Each identity belongs to one entry or component in the whole file. package (optional, on an entry or a component): the product's registry package as ecosystem:name, lowercase ecosystem; it is one more identity of that product, listed nowhere else. An MCP server, client, SDK or tool is an entry of its own, named by its package name, with ecosystem mcp (the key a member's inventory lists MCP servers under), so a post names what a member installs; there is no entry for MCP as a whole. formerly (optional, on an entry): names its product had in an earlier version of this file; a thread posted under one of them stays that advisory's thread. components (optional): a list of {name, match?, package?, authority?}, each a public product identity a member's inventory can report; a text that equals a component's identity resolves to that component, which is the product a post names, its upgrade target and its thread key. An entry has match, package, components or more than one. Entry and component names are unique across the file; a component may carry its own entry's name. authority (optional, on an entry or a component; a component's list replaces its entry's, and an empty list means none): URL prefixes, host/ or host/path/, lowercase, where that product's own vendor publishes advisories; a github.com prefix names one repository (github.com/owner/repo/), never an owner. A source is authoritative for a product only when it is on spec/primary-domains.json and under one of these prefixes; the CISA KEV catalog the poster fetched is authoritative for any product. A product with no authority posts from KEV only. Version 6 is the only version a reader accepts.",
  "entries": [
